# Test: both of them swaying while the bar drifts
# The third light-sensor run. As the second, and now the bar moves too - not its whole
# travel, but the narrow sweep that keeps the pair roughly in front of each other. Three
# things moving, all the movement a real encounter has.
# Thirty seconds.

0 The bar slides until male 1 is at his meeting point with female 1, and the test waits for it.
0 His ring comes on, steady.
0 Three things start together: her sway, his sway, and the bar drifting back and forth around that meeting point rather than crossing the rail.
0 The bar's drift is the slowest of the three, so it reads as the pair sliding gently past each other while both of them turn.
30 Every reading from all three females is written down throughout.
0 This is the closest of the first three runs to what the installation actually does, minus the blinking. If her sensor catches him for long enough here, it can catch a whole pattern in the real thing.
0 It is also the run where female 2 and female 3 stop being pure comparison: the bar's drift can bring him within sight of a neighbour. A reading on one of them is not necessarily noise.
0 The run ends itself after thirty seconds. All three movements stop, his ring goes out, the bar stays where it stands.
